  {
    "episode_id": 90146,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90146.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "Very good question now, just for background for the listeners. When I was 20, I created my first small business. Anybody in a small business will realize that the first to work, last to leave, last to be paid. And what we designed here was government came in and told business they had to shut down for the health and good of everybody else. So they have no income. And they only have one choice. Either they lay their employees off and they don't pay their rent. So we designed a program that would apply, if you put 75% of it to pay your employees, even if they're home, Pay your rent, pay your utilities, and we take two and a half times of your expenses for the next two months, a liquidity problem. We'd keep you afloat and that would be a grant. You, you wouldn't pay that back. Right. It was a very good program. It was so good that in 14 days, we did as many loans as the small business administration has done in the last 14 years. So you know the need of it. Yeah. So once we passed this and it started moving, We realized that $349 billion was not going to last long. But here we're home with COVID and everything else going on. So they have a provision inside both our bodies that a unanimous consent. If both sides agree, it can pass. You have what it's called a pro forma. Two weeks prior, April 7th, knowing long before this money would run out, we did a one-page bill. It doesn't change any policy. We're voting on the exact same thing. We just voted a couple of weeks ago. And everybody voted for to add more money to it. The Democrats literally sent somebody to the chambers to stand up and say, I object. And you know what happened? Just a few short weeks later, exactly what we said would happen. It ran out of money. And we tried again. And the Democrats said they objected.",
